import java.lang.reflect.Method;

public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int n = 4; // number of steps
        System.out.println(countWaysToClimbStairs(n));
    }

    public static long countWaysToClimbStairs(int n) throws Exception {
        Class<?> clazz = Class.forName("DynamicStaircaseSolution");
        Method method = clazz.getMethod("countWays", int.class);
        return (long) method.invoke(null, n);
    }
}

class DynamicStaircaseSolution {

    public static long countWays(int n) {
        long[] dp = new long[n + 1];
        dp[0] = 1;
        dp[1] = 1;
        for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++) {
            dp[i] = dp[i - 1] + dp[i - 2];
        }
        return dp[n];
    }

}

```

This Java code uses reflection and dynamic class loading to achieve the desired functionality. The `countWaysToClimbStairs` method is implemented as a dynamic class, and it uses an array of long values (`dp`) to store the number of ways to climb each step. The solution uses a simple iterative approach to calculate the number of ways to climb the staircase.

